vmjail
======

Run programs in a tiny Linux VM with a single command. This project aims to
make running processes in a VM as easy as running them in a more lightweight
sandbox like those using namespaces (e.g. nsjail).

A local Linux microVM sandbox for running an OCI container with explicit
host-directory mounts and host/guest observability. Cloud Hypervisor supplies
the VM, virtiofsd serves the shared directories, and a guest helper runs the
container with crun. The default kernel boots directly as an uncompressed
ELF through PVH, with an uncompressed initramfs and performance-oriented
kernel compiler optimization.

This repository is being reduced from darkaifactory. The distributed control
plane, web interface, remote client/MCP facade and workspace CAS have been deleted. The remaining Go packages compile and pass the default test suite.
The Go module is github.com/richiejp/vmjail. The vmjail CLI runs host commands
inside a VM with an nsjail-style argument subset; vmjail-sandboxd retains the
image/bundle interface. The guest helper is vmjail-agent. Configuration
variables use the VMJAIL_ prefix.

Run a host command
------------------

    nix build
    ./result/bin/vmjail -Mo --chroot / -- /bin/sh -c 'echo hello'

    # Allow writes only to a selected host directory.
    ./result/bin/vmjail -c / -B "$PWD:/work" --cwd /work -- /bin/sh

    # Retain guest IO/process traces and host resource logs.
    ./result/bin/vmjail -c / --observe-dir ./traces -- /bin/sh

This requires Linux, KVM, user/mount namespaces and a delegated cgroup (automatically obtained through systemd-run when available).
The selected root supplies executable files and libraries; no container image
is needed. --chroot / exposes ordinary readable host files. Host kernel
pseudo-filesystems are masked and /dev, /proc and /sys belong to the guest.
Without --chroot, the root is empty apart from guest system mounts and explicit
-R/-B mounts. See docs/nsjail-experiment.md for examples, limitations and the
implementation report.

Retained capabilities
---------------------

- Image pulling and OCI bundle preparation.
- Direct host-directory shares, with optional guest overlays.
- User namespaces, per-VM cgroups, resource limits and process cleanup.
- Host resource logs and guest eBPF file IO/process summaries.
- Guest control over vsock and local VM networking/exec primitives.

Writable direct mounts expose the selected host directories to workload writes.
The VM is the isolation boundary; sharing a directory is an explicit exception
to filesystem isolation. IO summaries aid debugging but are not a complete
filesystem audit.

Development
-----------

The Nix flake and .envrc describe the development toolchain. make dev builds
local artifacts and the CLI; make check runs the fast gates; make verify also
runs real VM tests, benchmark checks and fuzzing. See docs/nsjail-experiment.md
for development artifact environment variables and PLAN.md for current work.

Documentation
-------------

- docs/nsjail-experiment.md: local CLI usage, compatibility and experiment findings.
- ARCHITECTURE.md: the retained local runtime and component boundaries.
- OBSERVABILITY.md: host accounting, guest gadgets and diagnostic files.
- docs/usage/storage-paths.md: local state and cache locations.
- CONTRIBUTING.md: development and verification rules.
- PLAN.md: current scope, build status and next steps.

The earlier analysis and deletion reports under docs/archive/ record the reduction
process. They describe their audited revisions, not supported remote features.
Git history retains the original project and its removed documentation.
